<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Assam is a state of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> and Barak valleys, with its capital at Dispur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>. Ruled by the Ahom kingdom for about six centuries from the 13th, it was overrun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1817–26 and ceded to the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-yandabo">Treaty of Yandabo</a> in 1826. It is known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> plantations, its oil fields and the Assamese language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assam.jpg" alt="Assam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diganta Talukdar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beauty_of_Kaziranga_National_Park.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
